diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the HTTP application service on web_server_2 (10.200.0.5). Mechanism: clients can reach the server IP (ICMP succeeds), but there is no process listening on TCP/80, so SYNs to port 80 are refused immediately (RST), making browsers/curl fail instantly rather than time out. Localization: specific to web_server_2’s application layer; L3/L2 connectivity and routing in the fabric are healthy. Evidence: netstat on web_server_2 shows no 0.0.0.0:80 listener (only 127.0.0.11 ephemeral services), while web_server_0/1/3 each show “0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN.” From a user host, curl to http://10.200.0.5 returns immediately with no connect phase (total ~1.3 ms), consistent with connection refusal. Systemd reports apache2 is loaded/enabled but “inactive (dead)” on web_server_2. Root cause: the web service (apache2) is not running on web_server_2, leaving port 80 closed. Corrective fix: start or restart the HTTP service and ensure it remains enabled. On web_server_2, run: systemctl restart apache2 && systemctl status apache2 to confirm “active (running).” If it fails, check configuration and logs (e.g., /var/log/apache2/error.log) for syntax/bind errors; alternatively, start the intended web daemon (nginx vs apache2) per your build. After restoring the listener, re-validate from user hosts (curl/http) and confirm 0.0.0.0:80 LISTEN appears in netstat.
=== END ===